use std
into st_tex.amni

Imports: math, base64, gfx.codec_png, st_geom, st_body, st_draft, st_svg, st_guide and st_trace.

Facts every block shares. The picture being worked on lives in the globals of `st_trace`: `IW` and `IH` (its size in pixels), `IMG` (RGBA bytes, 4 per pixel, row by row), `MASK` (one byte per pixel, 1 for garment), `SMR`, `SMG` and `SMB` (the 3 by 3 smoothed red, green and blue of every pixel, 32-bit ints), `BX0`, `BX1`, `BY0` and `BY1` (the garment's bounding box), and `segment()`, which fills them from `IMG` and `IW` and `IH` and returns whether it found a garment. `st_trace` also has `avg_width(f0, f1)` and `width_at(f)`, the garment's pixel width over the rows between the fractions f0 and f1 of its height, or at the fraction f. `st_draft` has the global `D`, the drafted garment, with the reals `chest_w` and `waist_w` (finished circumferences in cm) and the bools `has_top`, `has_skirt` and `has_legs`. Reals are 64-bit floats. A whole division rounds down. The tile is square with a side of `TN` pixels.

```amni
let TN: int = 512;
var TX real[>..];
var TILE byte[>..];
var LUMA real[>..];
var ENG real[>..];
var ERO byte[>..];
var TWC real[>..];
var TWS real[>..];
var TEX_OK = false;
var TEX_MSG byte[>..];
var TEX_X0 = 0; var TEX_Y0 = 0; var TEX_SW = 0;
var TEX_CX = 0; var TEX_CY = 0; var TEX_CW = 0; var TEX_CH = 0;
var TEX_PX = 0.0; var TEX_PY = 0.0;
var TEX_CMPX = 0.0; var TEX_CM = 15.0; var TEX_AR = 1.0; var TEX_DX = 0; var TEX_DY = 0; var TEX_QX = 0.0; var TEX_QY = 0.0; var INTG real[>..];
var TEX_WRAP = 0.0; var TEX_INNER = 0.0; var TEX_RAWWRAP = 0.0; var TEX_LF0 = 0.0; var TEX_LF1 = 0.0;
```

`box_blur(a, w, h, rx, ry, odd)` blurs the list of reals a, which holds w times h values row by row, in place with a box filter of half-width rx across and ry down. A half-width of 0 leaves that direction alone. A value beyond an edge counts as the nearest edge value, unless the flag odd is true, then k places beyond the first value it counts as twice the first value minus the value k places inside (k at most the last index), and beyond the last value the same turned around, so a straight ramp stays straight at the edges. Use running sums so the cost does not depend on the radius, the horizontal pass first, then the vertical pass.

`tex_luma()` fills `LUMA`, one real per source pixel, with 0.299 times `SMR` plus 0.587 times `SMG` plus 0.114 times `SMB` of that pixel.

`tex_energy()` fills `ENG`, one real per source pixel, with the local gradient energy. First call `tex_luma`. Then take the Sobel gradient magnitude (the square root of gx squared plus gy squared) of `LUMA` at every pixel, a pixel beyond the edge counting as the nearest edge pixel, where gx is the sum over the three rows y minus 1, y and y plus 1 of weight 1, 2, 1 times (the pixel one to the right minus the pixel one to the left), and gy is the same turned a quarter. Finally `box_blur` that magnitude in both directions with half-width the larger of 3 and (the smaller of `IW` and `IH`, divided by 80), odd false.

`tex_eroded()` fills `ERO`, one byte per source pixel, with 1 where every `MASK` pixel in the square window of half-width er around it is 1 and the whole window lies inside the image, else 0, where er is the larger of 2 and (the smaller of `IW` and `IH`, divided by 34). Do it as two passes of running counts of the zero pixels, a horizontal one into a temporary byte list and then a vertical one, so the cost does not depend on er.

`tex_square(mult)` returns the corner x, the corner y and the side of the largest axis-aligned square of pixels that all have `ERO` equal to 1 and `ENG` at most mult times the mean `ENG` over the pixels with `ERO` equal to 1. Use the dynamic program where a qualifying pixel's value is 1 plus the smallest of its values above, to the left and above-left (0 for a pixel that does not qualify), keeping the largest value and the bottom-right pixel it was found at (the first in row-major order on a tie), and keeping only two rows of values. The corner is that pixel minus side minus 1 in each direction. When nothing qualifies it returns 0, 0 and 0.

`tex_pick()` sets `TEX_X0`, `TEX_Y0` and `TEX_SW` to the square used for the fabric and returns whether it is at least 16 pixels wide. It calls `tex_energy` and `tex_eroded`, then tries `tex_square` with the multipliers 1.5, 2.2, 3.2 and 4.7 in that order, remembering the largest square seen (the earlier one on a tie) and stopping at the first one whose side reaches the target, which is a quarter of the square root of the number of `MASK` pixels equal to 1. It keeps the remembered square.

`tex_period(p)` finds the repeat length, in samples, of the profile p (a list of reals), or 0 when it is not periodic. Let n be the length of p, subtract the mean from every entry, and let v be the mean of the squares; no period when v is 0. For every lag k from 1 up to 7 times n divided by 10 (whole division), the autocorrelation r of k is the sum over j from 0 below n minus k of entry j times entry j plus k, divided by (n minus k) times v. Let k0 be the first lag where r is below 0; no period when there is none. A peak is a lag k above k0 and below the last lag where r of k is at least r of k minus 1 and at least r of k plus 1. Let top be the highest r among the peaks; no period when there is no peak or top is below 0.45. Take the smallest peak whose r is at least 0.85 times top and refine it by parabolic interpolation: k plus 0.5 times (r of k minus 1 minus r of k plus 1) divided by (r of k minus 1 minus 2 times r of k plus r of k plus 1), or just k when that denominator is 0. Return that refined lag.

`tex_snap_len(p, s)` returns the crop length for a profile p of length s, and the period found. With q the result of `tex_period` of p: when q is at least 3, m is the larger of 1 and the whole part of s divided by q, and the length is the smaller of s and the nearest whole number to m times q; otherwise the length is s and the period 0. It returns the length and q.

`tex_snap()` narrows the picked square to a crop rectangle holding whole pattern repeats and sets `TEX_CX`, `TEX_CY`, `TEX_CW`, `TEX_CH`, `TEX_PX`, `TEX_PY`, `TEX_AR` and the periods found in source pixels `TEX_QX` and `TEX_QY` (0 when none). The column profile has `TEX_SW` entries, entry i being the mean of `LUMA` over the square's rows in column `TEX_X0` plus i; the row profile is the same turned a quarter. `TEX_CW` and the period come from `tex_snap_len` of the column profile and `TEX_SW`, `TEX_CH` and its period from the row profile. `TEX_CX` is `TEX_X0` plus half of (`TEX_SW` minus `TEX_CW`), whole division, and `TEX_CY` likewise. `TEX_PX` is the column period times `TN` divided by `TEX_CW` and `TEX_PY` is the row period times `TN` divided by `TEX_CH` (both 0 when the period is 0). `TEX_AR` is `TEX_CH` divided by `TEX_CW`.

`tex_integral()` fills `INTG` with the summed-area table of `LUMA` over the picture: (`IW` plus 1) times (`IH` plus 1) values, entry (x, y) being the sum of `LUMA` over the columns before x and the rows before y.

`tex_box_mean(x, y, w, h)` is the mean of `LUMA` over the box of w by h pixels with top-left corner (x, y), the box clamped into the picture, read from `INTG`; it is 1 when the clamped box is empty.

`tex_strip_cost(x, y, len, across, period, shift)` says how badly the pattern fails to repeat after period whole pixels, for the strip of `IMG` starting at (x, y) and running len pixels. When across is true it compares the four columns x plus k, k from 0 to 3, over the rows y to y plus len minus 1, with the columns x plus period plus k over the same rows moved down by shift; when across is false it compares the four rows y plus k with the rows y plus period plus k over the columns x to x plus len minus 1 moved right by shift. Every source position is clamped into the picture. To ignore uneven lighting, a gain g is found first: q is the nearest whole number to `TEX_QX` when across is true and to `TEX_QY` otherwise (at least 1), and g is the `tex_box_mean` of the window q wide and len tall at (x, y) when across (len wide and q tall when not) divided by the `tex_box_mean` of the same window moved by (period, shift) when across (by (shift, period) when not), or 1 when that mean is 0. For each of the four strips and each of the three colour channels, the differences d of the pixel minus g times its partner are taken, and the cost is the mean over all of them of d squared.

`tex_refine_h()` searches the horizontal repeat and does nothing when `TEX_QX` is 0. The period in source pixels is per, `TEX_QX`, and m0 is the larger of 1 and the whole part of `TEX_SW` divided by per. For each repeat count m from m0 down to the larger of 1 and m0 minus 1, with c the nearest whole number to m times per and e the larger of 3 and the nearest whole number to 0.02 times c, try every length w from c minus e to c plus e that is at most `TEX_SW` (and at least 8), and every x from `TEX_X0` to `TEX_X0` plus `TEX_SW` minus w: the score is `tex_strip_cost` of (x, `TEX_CY`, `TEX_CH`, true, w, `TEX_DY`) times (1 plus 0.04 times (m0 minus m)). Keep the lowest score; then `TEX_CX` and `TEX_CW` are its x and w and `TEX_PX` is `TN` divided by its m.

`tex_refine_v()` is the same turned a quarter: it does nothing when `TEX_QY` is 0, per is `TEX_QY`, m0 is the larger of 1 and the whole part of `TEX_SW` divided by per, lengths h come from `TEX_CH`, starts y from `TEX_Y0` to `TEX_Y0` plus `TEX_SW` minus h, the score is `tex_strip_cost` of (`TEX_CX`, y, `TEX_CW`, false, h, `TEX_DX`) times the same factor, and it sets `TEX_CY`, `TEX_CH` and `TEX_PY`.

`tex_refine_shear()` handles a photo that is slightly tilted. When `TEX_QX` is above 0, `TEX_DY` becomes the whole number d from minus 4 to 4 with the lowest `tex_strip_cost` of (`TEX_CX`, `TEX_CY`, `TEX_CH`, true, `TEX_CW`, d), the first one found on a tie when trying d in the order 0, 1, minus 1, 2, minus 2 and so on; when `TEX_QY` is above 0, `TEX_DX` becomes the d from minus 4 to 4 with the lowest `tex_strip_cost` of (`TEX_CX`, `TEX_CY`, `TEX_CW`, false, `TEX_CH`, d), tried in the same order.

`tex_refine()` tunes the crop so the pattern joins itself when wrapped. It calls `tex_integral`, sets `TEX_DX` and `TEX_DY` to 0, then twice calls `tex_refine_h`, `tex_refine_v` and `tex_refine_shear` in that order, and finally sets `TEX_AR` to `TEX_CH` divided by `TEX_CW`.

`tex_bilinear(fx, fy, c)` is the bilinear sample of channel c (0 red, 1 green, 2 blue) of `IMG` at the source position (fx, fy), where pixel (x, y) has its centre at (x plus 0.5, y plus 0.5); the position is clamped into the image and the four neighbours are blended.

`tex_resample()` fills `TX` with `TN` times `TN` pixels of 3 reals (red, green, blue, 0 to 255), row by row, resampled from the crop of `IMG`, which is a parallelogram: its top-left corner is (`TEX_CX`, `TEX_CY`), going right across the tile moves by (`TEX_CW`, `TEX_DY`) and going down moves by (`TEX_DX`, `TEX_CH`). A tile position (u, v), each from 0 to 1, is the source point (`TEX_CX` plus u times `TEX_CW` plus v times `TEX_DX`, `TEX_CY` plus v times `TEX_CH` plus u times `TEX_DY`). Tile pixel (i, j) is the mean of `tex_bilinear` taken at the source points of an nx by ny grid of equal cells, the cell a, b of that grid having u equal to (i plus (a plus 0.5) divided by nx) divided by `TN` and v equal to (j plus (b plus 0.5) divided by ny) divided by `TN`, where nx is `TEX_CW` divided by `TN` rounded up (at least 1) and ny is the same for `TEX_CH`.

`tex_window(q)` is the blur window in tile pixels for a pattern period q: `TN` divided by 3 (whole division) when q is 0, otherwise q times the larger of 1 and the nearest whole number to (`TN` divided by 3) divided by q, so the window holds whole repeats and averages the pattern away.

`tex_lowpass(a)` returns the blurred luminance of a tile a (3 reals per pixel, `TN` times `TN` pixels) as a list of reals: the luminance of every pixel (0.299 red plus 0.587 green plus 0.114 blue) blurred twice with `box_blur` (odd true), the half-width across being the larger of 1 and half of `tex_window` of `TEX_PX` (rounded to a whole number) and the half-width down the same from `TEX_PY`.

`tex_flatten()` removes the lighting from `TX`. Remember the mean of each channel of `TX`, take `tex_lowpass` of `TX` and its mean, and divide the red, green and blue of every pixel by its blurred luminance divided by that mean, the divisor at least 0.2. Then multiply each channel by its remembered mean divided by its new mean (so the mean colour is restored) and clamp every value to 0 to 255.

`tex_lowrange(a)` is the low-frequency luminance range of a tile a (3 reals per pixel, `TN` times `TN` pixels): the largest minus the smallest value of `tex_lowpass` of a.

`tex_jumps(a)` returns two reals for a tile a (3 reals per pixel, `TN` times `TN` pixels): the mean absolute colour jump across the wrap edges, and the mean absolute colour jump between neighbouring pixels inside the tile. The jump between two pixels is the mean over red, green and blue of the absolute difference. The wrap value is the mean over the `TN` row pairs (the last column against the first column of every row) and the `TN` column pairs (the last row against the first row of every column). The inside value is the mean over every pair of horizontal neighbours and every pair of vertical neighbours.

`tex_twiddles()` fills `TWC` and `TWS`, each `TN` divided by 2 reals long, with the cosine and sine of 2 pi times k divided by `TN` for k from 0, but only when they are empty.

`fft_line(re, im, start, step, inv)` transforms in place the `TN` complex values whose real parts are the entries start, start plus step, start plus 2 times step and so on of the list re, and the imaginary parts the same entries of im. Use the iterative radix-2 FFT: first swap entries into bit-reversed order, then for every length 2, 4, 8 up to `TN` combine the halves with the twiddle factors from `TWC` and `TWS` (entry j times `TN` divided by length), whose sine is subtracted for the forward transform and added when inv is true. There is no scaling.

`fft2(re, im, inv)` transforms the `TN` times `TN` complex image in re and im (row by row) in place: `tex_twiddles`, then `fft_line` along every row, then along every column, and when inv is true divide every real and imaginary part by `TN` times `TN`.

`tex_periodic()` makes `TX` tile seamlessly with the periodic-plus-smooth decomposition, channel by channel. For one channel u (`TN` times `TN` values) build the boundary image v, zero everywhere except: in column 0, v is u of the last column minus u of column 0, and in the last column, u of column 0 minus u of the last column; in row 0, v gets added u of the last row minus u of row 0, and in the last row u of row 0 minus u of the last row (corner pixels get both additions). Transform v with `fft2`; for every frequency (a across, b down) other than (0, 0) divide it by 2 cos(2 pi a divided by `TN`) plus 2 cos(2 pi b divided by `TN`) minus 4, and set the (0, 0) value to 0; transform back with `fft2` (inverse) to get the smooth part s, and replace u by u minus the real part of s. Finally clamp every value of `TX` to 0 to 255.

`tex_finish()` writes `TILE` from `TX`: 4 bytes per pixel, the red, green and blue rounded to the nearest whole number and clamped to 0 to 255, and alpha 255.

`tex_scale()` estimates the centimetres per source pixel from the garment's pixel width and the drafted garment's real width, or returns 0 when it cannot. Use `D`: when `has_top` and neither `has_skirt` nor `has_legs`, the pixel width is `avg_width` of 0.5 and 0.7 and the real width is `chest_w` divided by 2; when `has_top` and `has_skirt`, the pixel width is the smallest `width_at` over the fractions 0.25, 0.26 and so on up to 0.55 and the real width is `waist_w` divided by 2; when `has_skirt` or `has_legs` without a top, the pixel width is `avg_width` of 0.02 and 0.06 and the real width is `waist_w` divided by 2; otherwise 0. It also returns 0 when the pixel width is below 4 or the real width is not above 0. The result is the real width divided by the pixel width.

`tex_build()` turns the picture in `IMG`, `IW` and `IH` into the fabric tile and returns whether it worked, always setting `TEX_OK` and `TEX_MSG` (a one-line status). Steps: `segment`; when it finds no garment the message is "Could not find a garment against the background. Use a picture with a plain, light background and the whole garment in frame." and it fails; `tex_pick`; when no square is found the message is "No clean patch of fabric was found. Seams, buttons and collars fill the garment, so try a picture with more plain fabric showing." and it fails; `tex_snap`; `tex_refine`; `TEX_CMPX` from `tex_scale`; `TEX_CM` is `TEX_CW` times `TEX_CMPX` when `TEX_CMPX` is above 0 and that is from 3 to 60, otherwise `TEX_CMPX` becomes 0 and `TEX_CM` 15; `tex_resample`; `TEX_RAWWRAP` is the first value of `tex_jumps` of `TX` and `TEX_LF0` is `tex_lowrange` of `TX`; `tex_flatten`; `tex_periodic`; `TEX_WRAP` and `TEX_INNER` are the two values of `tex_jumps` of `TX` and `TEX_LF1` is `tex_lowrange` of `TX`; `tex_finish`. On success the message is "Fabric tile ready, " followed by the tile width as inches with one decimal, " in (" and centimetres with one decimal, " cm) wide, " and then "scaled from the garment's size." when `TEX_CMPX` is above 0, otherwise "at the default size; set the real size in the 3D tab."

`tex_png(out)` appends the PNG file of the tile (red, green and blue only, `TN` times `TN`) to the byte list out, using `codec_png::encode` with 3 channels.

`tex_data_url(out)` clears the byte list out and fills it with "data:image/png;base64," followed by the base64 of the bytes `tex_png` gives. `base64::encode` takes the list to append to first and the bytes to encode second, so call it with out first.

`tex_overlay_svg(out)` appends an svg to the byte list out for drawing over the picture in image pixels: viewBox "0 0 IW IH" with the numbers filled in, preserveAspectRatio "none", containing a rect of class "fx" for the picked square (`TEX_X0`, `TEX_Y0`, side `TEX_SW`) and a polygon of class "fc" for the crop parallelogram, its points being (`TEX_CX`, `TEX_CY`), then that plus (`TEX_CW`, `TEX_DY`), then that plus (`TEX_DX`, `TEX_CH`), then (`TEX_CX` plus `TEX_DX`, `TEX_CY` plus `TEX_CH`), each point as x, a comma and y, separated by spaces.
